    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense for the Creator field, but I'm also worried about the \"Subject\" and \"Coverage\" fields for some of these pieces. Some of the maps depict regions or landmarks that don't quite align with standard gazetteers, or they feature strange, non-specific iconography that makes it hard to assign a definitive controlled vocabulary term. \n\nHow should I approach mapping these ambiguous or \"unclassifiable\" elements in the metadata so that they don't just end up looking like errors in the database? Is there a way to capture that sense of uncertainty or 'obscurity' within the schema?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Within Dublin Core metadata, to address ambiguous or unclassifiable elements without introducing errors, you can utilize a combination of controlled vocabularies and human-readable notes. For example, you would first attempt to use the closest available term within the standard controlled vocabularies, and if none are suitable, include an alternate term in square brackets to reflect the uncertainty, e.g., [\"Place Unknown\"], [\"Landmark Unclassifiable\"]. This allows you to document the obscurity while still keeping the metadata within the acceptable parameters.\n\n\nIn fields where precise categorization is difficult, it is also beneficial to provide a detailed human-readable note that explains the complexity of the map's content or the uncertainty of the terms used. This could be included as additional archival information or a sibling to the primary metadata, ensuring it doesn't interfere with the controlled vocabulary or automate processes that require standard terms. If consistent among all such cases, these practices can both maintain interoperability and uphold the integrity of the historical record.",
      "source": "target",
